course: dictionary
lesson: 992
title: Words that protect
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A bodyguard is a person whose job is to keep an important person safe from harm. The word has two parts: body, meaning a person, and guard, meaning to keep watch over something.
explain: Read this sentence: «Every lie needs a bodyguard of new lies, because people ask the next question.» The new lies are there to protect the first lie from being found out. So a bodyguard is something that protects.
explain: When you meet a new word, ask what job it does in the sentence. Then put your meaning in place of the word and check that it still makes sense.
fact: A bodyguard is a person whose job is to protect an important or famous person from being attacked or harmed.
fact: A group of such guards is also called a bodyguard.
fact: «Personal bodyguard» means a bodyguard who looks after one person.
fact: «Hire a bodyguard» means to pay someone to protect you.
fact: «Surrounded by bodyguards» means there are guards standing all around someone to keep them safe.
exercise: «Every lie needs a bodyguard of new lies, because people ask the next question.» In this sentence, what job does the bodyguard of lies do? | Asks the next question | Tells the truth to everyone | *Protects the first lie from being found out | Makes the lies louder
exercise: The famous singer walked into the hall, surrounded by bodyguards. What does this suggest? | The hall was empty and quiet | The singer was about to sing a song | The singer was alone and lost | *The singer was guarded closely by people keeping them safe
exercise: Which sentence best describes a bodyguard? | *A person whose job is to protect an important person from harm | A teacher who marks spelling tests | A shop assistant who sells tickets | A machine that makes lights
test: Ruby's pop star has a personal bodyguard. What is a personal bodyguard? | A ticket seller at a concert | A famous person who likes to sing | *A person who protects one important person from harm | A group of fans waiting for an autograph
test: «He hired a bodyguard before the big trip.» What did he hire? | A map of the city | *Someone to protect him from harm | A bus driver for the school trip | A cook for the journey
exam: A famous politician hires a bodyguard. Why? | To make the politician famous | To vote for the politician | *To keep the politician safe from being attacked or harmed | To carry the politician's bags
